# 📐 Using conventions

`docs/conventions/` holds the coding norms of this repository as **atoms**: one norm per markdown file, grouped by
directory (`agent`, `programming`, `typescript`, `react`, `css`, `testing`, `eslint`, `markdown`, `unused`). Each atom
declares `id`, `emoji` and `applies`, and has fixed sections `📐 Norm`, `🔍 Detect`, `🔧 Fix`, `📝 Examples`. The format
itself is described in `docs/conventions/README.md`.

## Loading

Before writing or reviewing code, read the atoms of the groups that match the target files:

- any file — `agent`, `programming`
- `*.ts`, `*.tsx` — `typescript`
- `*.tsx` — `react`, plus `react/view-state` when splitting a component into `Entry`/`State`/`View`
- `*.scss` — `css`
- `*.test.ts` — `testing`
- `*.md` — `markdown`
- `eslint-disable` comments — `eslint`
- dead code hunts and cleanups — `unused`

Read the files from disk with Glob and Read; never answer from memory of what a convention says. Enforce an atom only
when its `applies` glob matches the path (`*` = any path) and the pattern it talks about actually exists here.

## Applying

- Atoms are the source of truth. They outrank your habits, your preference, and patterns already present in the code —
  if existing code contradicts an atom, the code is wrong.
- Verify with each atom's `🔍 Detect` section using grep or a file read, not by assumption.
- Report a violation as one line starting with that atom's `emoji` field, then the file and place, the problem, and the
  fix. Do not paste atom bodies or cite atom ids in the report.
- Never edit an atom to make current code look correct. Atoms change only when the user asks to change a convention.

## Adding

Add `docs/conventions/<group>/<kebab-name>.md` (or `<group>/<topic>/<kebab-name>.md`) in the atom format, with `id`
mirroring the path without `.md`. A new group is just a new subdirectory. Nothing else in the directory needs an edit.
